  "task": {
    "name": "groom_how_shaped_ac matches bare substrings \u2014 outcome language like \"renamed\" flags as HOW-shaped, dropping a runnable story at intake",
    "slug": "issue-1758",
    "story_path": null,
    "story_text": "## Observed behavior\n\nOn an adopter repo (hdp, sprint 3aa441a6aa78, 2026-07-17), the how-shaped-AC groom gate blocked a runnable story and it ended in DROPPED_AFTER_FIX plus operator intervention. The story's acceptance bullet described a user *outcome* \u2014 \"A renamed or closely related exercise still shows its prior performance history on the card\" \u2014 pure outcome language about an entity that had been renamed. The gate raised a blocking `groom_how_shaped_ac` finding on both the original body and the intake auto-fix candidate (`rerun gate still failing; candidate posted as issue comment for operator review`), so the story was dropped from the sprint. The gate's own comment states it is intentionally conservative because \"false positives would cause the grooming check to drop runnable stories\" \u2014 that is precisely the outcome that occurred.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe grooming gate exists to catch acceptance criteria that prescribe *how* to implement a change, not to react to ordinary domain vocabulary that happens to share a word stem with an implementation verb. A bullet describing an observable outcome about an entity \u2014 a thing that was renamed, extracted, or refactored \u2014 must not be treated as an instruction *to* rename, extract, or refactor code, regardless of the product domain or the repo's language. The gate's matching must not fire on inflected or derived forms (participles, plurals, adjectival forms) of its trigger vocabulary when those forms appear inside otherwise outcome-shaped prose; it should continue to fire on imperative directives that instruct changing an identifier's name, pulling logic into a helper, or restructuring a code path. In short, the classifier must distinguish outcome language that mentions a domain entity from an implementation directive that acts on a code entity, so that the conservative-by-design gate does not drop stories it was never meant to catch.\n\n## Diagnosis\n\n- **Observed symptom:** hdp issue #141's AC bullet \"A renamed or closely related exercise still shows its prior performance history on the card\" produced a blocking `groom_how_shaped_ac` finding on both the original body and the intake auto-fix candidate, dropping the story.\n- **Evidence:** `_HOW_TOKENS` includes a bare verb token whose stem is `rename`, and `_HOW_RE` (src/theforge/intake/groom.py:27-44) compiles the token list with `re.escape` and `re.IGNORECASE` but no word-boundary anchors. Confirmed repro: `_HOW_RE.search(\"A renamed or closely related exercise still shows its prior performance history on the card\")` returns a match on the substring `rename`. The same substring behavior reproduces for other bare stems (`refactored`, `extracted`/`extraction`, `subclasses`, `renames`).\n- **Ruled out:** Not the bullet extractor \u2014 `extract_contextual_bullets` located the AC bullet correctly. Not the example-block directive path \u2014 the match comes solely from the token regex. Not adopter misconfiguration \u2014 the same token list ships as the built-in default.\n- **Confirmed cause:** Unanchored substring matching lets inflected/derived forms of the bare verb stems match. The trailing-space tokens (`\"use the \"`, `\"class \"`, etc.) are partially guarded by their trailing space; the bare verb stems are not. To this regex, a bullet stating an observable outcome about an entity that *was* renamed is indistinguishable from an instruction *to* rename something.\n- **Affected code path:** `theforge/intake/groom.py` `_HOW_TOKENS`/`_HOW_RE` \u2192 `_ac_lines_look_how_shaped` \u2192 `groom_check` \u2192 sprint intake BLOCK \u2192 auto-fix rerun (same gate) \u2192 DROPPED_AFTER_FIX.\n",
    "github_issue": 1758,
    "fix_ready": false,
